::: {.doc-subject}

Compute the Weisfeiler-Lehman hash of the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L27161-L27343){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash(
    node_attr=None,
    edge_attr=None,
    iterations=3,
    digest_size=16,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Weisfeiler-Lehman graph hash](../assets/graphs/weisfeiler-lehman-graph-hash.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Two drawings of a triangle with a one-edge tail, one labeled A to D and one labeled w to z. Node colors mark the classes that neighborhood aggregation finds: the degree-3 node (C, x) in accent, the two other triangle nodes (A, B and y, z) in teal, and the tail end (D, w) in violet. Both graphs reduce to the same class counts, so the card shows the same hash, starting c0454391, for each, and equal is True." width="100%"}

The Weisfeiler-Lehman (WL) hash summarizes a graph's structure in a short hexadecimal
string. Isomorphic graphs always get the same hash, so different hashes prove that two
graphs differ. The converse does not hold: some non-isomorphic graphs (for example
many regular graphs of the same degree) share a hash. It is the standard way to group
or deduplicate large collections of graphs, such as molecules, before running exact
isomorphism tests within each group, and it underlies the WL graph kernels used in
machine learning.

Each node starts with a label (its degree, or the attribute named by `node_attr=`),
then, `iterations` times, replaces it by a hash of its own label and the sorted labels
of its neighbors (prefixed by the edge attribute named by `edge_attr=`, if given). The
graph hash is a hash of the counts of labels across all rounds. The result is
identical to NetworkX's `weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash` for the same graph (NetworkX
3.5 or later), except for directed graphs hashed with `edge_attr=`. There, each
neighbor label keeps its successor or predecessor prefix, following the fix in
NetworkX pull request #8946. NetworkX releases without that fix drop the prefix, so
an in-star and an out-star get the same hash.

Parallel edges count once and a self-loop makes a node its own neighbor, as in a
NetworkX `Graph` or `DiGraph`.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[node_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional node attribute used as the initial label (converted with `str()`). Every
    node must have it.

<code>[edge_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional edge attribute whose value prefixes each neighbor's label. Every edge must
    have it.

<code>[iterations]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[3]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Number of neighborhood aggregation rounds (default 3). More rounds look further
    from each node.

<code>[digest_size]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[16]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Size of the BLAKE2b digests, in bytes (the hash string has twice as many hex
    characters). Default 16.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The hash, as a hexadecimal string.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If `iterations` is not positive, or a node or edge lacks a named attribute.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

This is the WL subtree hash of Shervashidze et al. (2011). With degree labels, the
first round only re-encodes the degrees, so NetworkX (and this method) runs one round
fewer in that case. Each round costs $O(E \log \Delta)$ for sorting neighbor labels. Two
graphs get equal hashes exactly when 1-WL color refinement cannot tell them apart within
the given number of rounds (barring hash collisions).

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~weisfeiler_lehman_subgraph_hashes`) 

:   The hashes of each node's neighborhood.

[](`~canonical_labeling`) 

:   An exact canonical form, where equal forms prove isomorphism.

[](`~is_isomorphic`) 

:   Confirm a match within a group of equal hashes.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Relabeling a graph does not change its hash:

::: {#b95b5742 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

a = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A"), ("C", "D")])
b = ec.Graph.from_edges([("z", "y"), ("y", "x"), ("x", "z"), ("w", "x")])

a.weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash() == b.weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
True
```
:::
:::


Equal hashes are not proof of isomorphism. A six-node cycle and two triangles are both
2-regular, so neighborhood aggregation never tells their nodes apart and the hashes
collide, while `is_isomorphic()` sees the difference:

::: {#b14581c5 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
hexagon = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(6)
two_triangles = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3).add_cycle_graph(3)

(
    hexagon.weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash() == two_triangles.weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash(),
    hexagon.is_isomorphic(two_triangles),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Finding duplicate molecules

A collection of molecules gathered from several sources can list the same molecule more
than once, under different numberings. Grouping by hash finds the candidates for
duplicates without comparing every pair. Here caffeine is added a second time,
renumbered by the scheme for purines. By shape alone, three groups hold more than one
molecule:

::: {#9f898550 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
molecules = ec.molecules()
purine = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("N1", "C2"), ("C2", "N3"), ("N3", "C4"), ("C4", "C5"), ("C5", "C6"), ("C6", "N1"),
     ("C4", "N9"), ("N9", "C8"), ("C8", "N7"), ("N7", "C5"),
     ("C2", "O10"), ("C6", "O11"), ("N1", "C12"), ("N3", "C13"), ("N7", "C14")]
)
purine.set_node_attrs("element", {atom: atom[0] for atom in purine.get_node_df()["label"]})
purine.set_edge_attrs("bond", "single")
purine.set_edge_attrs(
    "bond", "double", edges=[("C4", "C5"), ("N9", "C8"), ("C2", "O10"), ("C6", "O11")]
)
molecules["caffeine_purine"] = purine


def duplicates(**kwargs):
    groups = {}
    for name, molecule in molecules.items():
        groups.setdefault(molecule.weisfeiler_lehman_graph_hash(**kwargs), []).append(name)
    return [names for names in groups.values() if len(names) > 1]


duplicates()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}
```
[['benzene', 'cyclohexane'],
 ['ethanol', 'dimethyl_ether'],
 ['caffeine', 'caffeine_purine']]
```
:::
:::


With `node_attr="element"`, ethanol and dimethyl ether separate, and with
`edge_attr="bond"` as well, so do benzene and cyclohexane, which differ only in their
bonds. Only the two numberings of caffeine are left:

